# Neon Portfolio - Cursor Rules

## Project Identity

This project is a premium interactive 3D portfolio.

It is NOT a traditional website.

It is NOT a game.

It is an immersive product experience built to showcase software engineering skills, product thinking, interaction design, and technical craftsmanship.

Every implementation must improve quality, maintainability, performance, and user experience.

---

# General Principles

Prioritize:

- Simplicity
- Readability
- Maintainability
- Performance
- Scalability
- Reusability

Never over-engineer.

Choose the simplest architecture that satisfies the requirements.

---

# Technology Stack

Always use:

- React
- TypeScript
- Vite
- React Three Fiber
- Three.js
- Drei
- Zustand
- GSAP
- Framer Motion
- Tailwind CSS

Also approved by TECHNICAL_SPEC (use when needed):

- Howler.js (event-driven audio)
- @react-three/postprocessing (bloom, vignette, noise, DoF, tone mapping)

Do not introduce new libraries without explaining why they are necessary.

---

# Architecture

Strictly follow:

- documentation/PRODUCT_VISION.md
- documentation/TECHNICAL_SPEC.md
- documentation/ARCHITECTURE.md
- Current phase documentation under documentation/phases/

Never violate the established architecture.

Respect separation of concerns.

---

# Component Rules

Each component has ONE responsibility.

Prefer many small components over large components.

Target:

100–200 lines per component.

Avoid components exceeding 300 lines unless justified.

---

# TypeScript

Always use strict typing.

Never use:

- any
- @ts-ignore

Prefer:

- interfaces
- utility types
- discriminated unions
- generics when appropriate

Every prop must be typed.

Every exported function must have an explicit return type when it improves readability.

---

# React Rules

Use:

Functional Components

React Hooks

Composition

Custom hooks

Avoid:

Class components

Prop drilling across many levels

Duplicated logic

Large useEffect blocks

Avoid unnecessary state.

---

# State Management

Global State

Use Zustand only.

Local UI state

Use React state.

Animation state

Never store inside Zustand.

Keep animation state inside GSAP or React Three Fiber.

---

# React Three Fiber

Never place business logic inside render loops.

Use useFrame only for continuous animation.

Dispose GPU resources correctly.

Reuse geometries.

Reuse materials.

Avoid unnecessary object creation.

Prefer instancing when many similar objects exist.

---

# Animation

GSAP

- Camera movement
- Scene transitions
- Timelines

Framer Motion

- HTML overlays
- UI animations

React Three Fiber

- Continuous floating
- Idle movement
- Particles

Animations must feel smooth.

Never use abrupt transitions.

---

# Performance

Performance is a feature.

Always consider:

- render count
- draw calls
- memory usage
- shader complexity
- asset size

Avoid unnecessary rerenders.

Memoize only when there is measurable benefit.

Lazy-load heavy assets.

---

# Folder Structure

Respect the existing architecture.

Do not create new folders unless necessary.

Place files in the correct domain.

Never mix UI, rendering, and business logic.

---

# Imports

Use path aliases.

Example

import { FloatingCard } from "@components/cards"

Never use deep relative imports like:

../../../../../

---

# Styling

Use Tailwind CSS.

Avoid inline styles.

Use design tokens from configuration.

Maintain spacing consistency.

---

# Naming

Components

PascalCase

Functions

camelCase

Variables

camelCase

Constants

UPPER_SNAKE_CASE

Files

PascalCase for React components.

camelCase or kebab-case for utilities depending on project convention.

Use descriptive names.

Never abbreviate important concepts.

---

# Magic Numbers

Never hardcode values.

Move configurable values into:

config/

Examples

Camera speed

Bloom intensity

Animation duration

Light intensity

Card spacing

Fog density

Colors

---

# Reusability

If logic is repeated twice,

consider extracting it.

If repeated three times,

extract it.

Build reusable systems instead of one-off solutions.

---

# Comments

Write comments only when they explain WHY.

Do not comment obvious code.

Example

Good:

// Smooth interpolation prevents motion sickness.

Bad:

// Increment x by one.

---

# Error Handling

Fail gracefully.

Provide fallback behavior.

Never allow runtime crashes because of missing assets.

Always validate external data.

---

# Accessibility

Support:

Keyboard navigation

Reduced motion

Semantic HTML

Readable contrast

Visible focus states

Accessibility is required.

---

# Assets

Optimize all assets.

Prefer:

AVIF

WebP

GLB

Compressed textures

Compressed audio

Never commit oversized assets.

---

# Code Quality

No duplicated logic.

No dead code.

No console.log in production.

No unused imports.

No unused variables.

No TODO comments without context.

---

# Git

Generate clean commits.

One feature per commit.

Keep pull requests focused.

---

# Before Writing Code

Always think through:

1. Is this reusable?
2. Is this the simplest solution?
3. Does it respect the architecture?
4. Will it scale?
5. Does it improve user experience?
6. Is it performant?

Only then implement.

---

# When Generating Code

Always:

- Explain architectural decisions.
- Generate production-ready code.
- Follow project conventions.
- Keep files modular.
- Preserve type safety.
- Optimize for readability first.
- Keep future expansion in mind.

Never sacrifice maintainability for cleverness.

---

# Project Goal

Every line of code should contribute to one objective:

Build a portfolio that convinces a recruiter to schedule an interview.

If a feature does not improve that goal, question whether it belongs in the project.